<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zhengtong Emperor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1427">1427</a>–<a href="/years/1464">1464</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Emperor Yingzong of Ming, Tianshun Emperor, Zhu Qizhen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Zhengtong Emperor, Zhu Qizhen, ruled Ming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> from 1435 to 1449 and again, as the Tianshun Emperor, from 1457 to 1464. Urged by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuch</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-zhen-eunuch">Wang Zhen</a>, he led a campaign against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirats</a> in 1449 and was captured by Esen at the Tumu Fortress. Released in 1450, he was kept under house arrest by his brother, the Jingtai Emperor, until a palace coup restored him in 1457.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zhengtong-emperor.jpg" alt="Zhengtong Emperor" width="320" height="428" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ming_Yingzong_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Ming Great Wall seen by visitors today is largely a 16th-century brick-faced work, built after the <b>Zhengtong Emperor</b> was captured by the Oirats at Tumu in 1449.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ming-empire">Ming Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>It could also be won by war, as the Yongle Emperor took it from his nephew in 1402, and lost by capture: when the Oirats seized the <b>Zhengtong Emperor</b> in 1449 his brother took the throne, and he won it back only by a coup in 1457.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ming-china">Social Hierarchy of Ming China</a></li><li><blockquote>Fearing a purge by the <b>Tianshun Emperor</b>, General Cao Qin and his troops rose in Beijing, set fire to gates of the Imperial City and killed several leading ministers.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-7">7 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Toghon and his son Esen built Oirat power in the 15th century; Esen defeated and captured the Ming emperor <b>Zhengtong</b> at Tumu in 1449 and briefly took the title of khan himself before being killed in 1455.</blockquote><a href="/state/four-oirat">Four Oirat</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.9,116.41&amp;year=1451" aria-label="Beijing on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7418 736 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Zhengtong Emperor"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7868 1006h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7868 1006h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.9,116.41&amp;year=1451">What was here in 1451? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
